AIRK @VERSION@ — Linux x86-64 offline installer

This package installs AIRK's native command-line client and local server. It
contains no VSIX, does not install an editor extension, and does not open VS Code.
The offline inference models and their license notices remain in the native
payload. The VS Code extension is an optional, separate download.

This Linux installer is unsigned. SHA256SUMS and the published release record
provide integrity/provenance checks; they are not a code-signing signature.
Download from AIRK's official release and verify its published checksum before
running any bundled executable. The installer also verifies its exact payload
and passes a pinned install-manifest digest to AIRK's native installation owner.

For an artifact published by the attestation-enabled GitHub release workflow,
also verify its GitHub build provenance before extracting or running it:

  gh attestation verify @ARCHIVE_NAME@ -R iokaio/airk \
    --signer-workflow iokaio/airk/.github/workflows/github-release.yml \
    --deny-self-hosted-runners

Check that verification succeeds for the expected official repository and
release workflow. A locally generated package does not acquire an attestation
merely by being packaged. This check uses GitHub; installation itself is offline.
Build provenance does not make this Linux executable OS-code-signed.
GitHub CLI reference: https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_attestation_verify

On Linux x86-64:

  tar -xzf @ARCHIVE_NAME@
  cd @DIRECTORY_NAME@
  ./install.sh

The guide reviews per-user installation without sudo, offers migration or a
fresh rebuild before upgrading saved indexes, then helps select search scope
and connect a supported coding agent. Sources stay read-only. Native setup
retains the authority for paths, state ownership, activation and indexing.

Keep the extracted package if installation is interrupted. Running install.sh
again resumes through the native installation owner; it does not delete old
state or substitute source folders. Retained failures may need review.

Existing client configuration and trust are not silently replaced. Linux
system certificate-store enrollment requires the separately documented
administrator process; this installer does not change OS trust or use sudo.
After installation, run `airk setup` for guided setup or `airk uninstall` for
native uninstall. Removing the extracted download alone does not uninstall AIRK.

Requirements: Linux x86-64, a POSIX shell and standard GNU sha256sum/find/coreutils.
No Python, jq, network download or editor is required by this installer.
